    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Reproduction in ant species takes place at the colony level, and daughter queens disperse after mating, two facts that make it difficult to track descent in ants. Genetic analysis can provide invaluable information about parent-offspring relationships in ants, making it possible to quantify reproductive success, a crucial measure for relating phenotype, fitness and environmental factors in ecology.\r\nThe Gordon Lab has been tracking a population of Pogonomyrmex barbatus colonies in a field site in the Chihuahuan desert in southeastern Arizona since 1985. Sequencing of the cox1 mitochondrial gene and five polymorphic microsatellite loci was performed on samples from 10 colonies, which were taken from the field in August 2013.\r\nLike other harvester ant populations, the P. barbatus colonies on the site show an asymmetric ratio of two interbred but genetically distinct lineages. The ratio for the 10 colonies sampled in this project was not significantly different from samples taken in 1999 and 2010.\r\nThe sequencing data collected in this project will enable the lab to properly identify colonies that have relocated their nests or for which marking tags have been lost. Additionally, the lab will be able to determine if the colonies sampled have parent or offspring colonies living on the site.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
